 {:name "recall-extraction-v1-3-strip-tex-fragments-and-packet-boilerplate"
  :level :loop
  :status :draft
  :body
  {:marker-kind :miss
   :observed-miss
   "The S4 receipt contained the useful normalized terms `convergence`, `weakly`, `hilbert`, and `liminf`, yet recall remained empty because TeX fragments and dispatch boilerplate diluted the specialized overlap."
   :receipt-query
   "a93A03 convergence infinity cdot langle lim_ rangle weakly converges sequence converge denote h_n-h hilbert inner apm-a93a03 select commit your part sorry they completion final liminf"
   :exact-stopword-additions
   ["cdot"
    "langle"
    "rangle"
    "denote"
    "select"
    "sorry"
    "commit"
    "your"
    "they"
    "part"]
   :tex-fragment-rule
   "Before tokenization, strip standalone TeX command fragments and delimiter names produced by backslash commands (for example `cdot`, `langle`, and `rangle`), while retaining semantic normalizations such as `L^2`→`L2` and arrows→`convergence`."
   :packet-rule
   "Apply the exact packet-boilerplate stopwords above to stdin-derived terms so workflow instructions cannot outrank mathematical terrain."
   :acceptance-replay
   "Re-run the S4 receipt query after v1.3 extraction; `math/weak-convergence-hilbert` should enter the candidate set from weak/convergence/Hilbert/liminf terms, while the removed fragments contribute no score."
   :boundary
   "This proposal fixes candidate pollution only. S4 separately showed that the surfaced memory would still need scope rejection because its bounded-under construction does not solve cobounded-under."}
